Umbra-Salsa

Creative Catalyst: It descends from carnival musicians who perform only during solar eclipses.

This genre is a violent reconciliation of the Caribbean dance floor and the cosmic void. It strips Timba of its sunny virtuosity, transmuting its polyrhythmic buoyancy into a sluggish, ceremonial march. The Neurofunk bass—usually a digital scalpel—is here repurposed as a subterranean, orchestral cello section, rumbling under sweeping cinematic brass arrangements that sound like they were recorded inside an ancient stone monument. It is the music of the Totalité, where the groove doesn’t propel you to dance, but keeps you pinned to the ground as the moon swallows the sun.

Genre DNA

*   Tempo & Pulse: 78–82 BPM. A "broken" syncopated swing that mimics the hesitation of a slowing heartbeat.
*   Harmonic Palette: Dark D-Lydian, microtonal shifting, and augmented chords that resolve into dissonant cluster-crashes.
*   Production Signatures: 
       Umbral Layering:* Micro-delaying every instrument by exactly 12 milliseconds to create a "sinking" sensation.
       Sub-Bass Orchestration:* The Neurofunk bass is EQ-carved to sit in the frequency range of a contrabassoon, playing orchestral counterpoint to the brass.
       Eclipse-Verb:* A custom reverb convolution profile captured inside a literal glass-lined bunker, designed to kill all high-end reflections.
*   Dynamics: A slow, terrifying swell that ignores traditional song structure, erupting into a "blaster-cacophony" only during the final 30 seconds of a piece.

Scene & Origin Lore

The Umbra-Salsa scene exists exclusively in high-altitude desert regions—specifically the Atacama—where "The Umbralites" congregate weeks before predicted solar events. They are musicians-turned-astronomers who wear vestments of reflective Mylar and volcanic glass. When the sky goes dark, the performance begins: an improvised ritual to keep the sun from vanishing entirely. They treat the eclipse not as a natural phenomenon, but as a technical failure of time itself. Fashion consists of "glitch-tailoring"—traditional tuxedo jackets sliced with laser cutters and stitched together with conductive copper wire that hums when the sun is 100% occluded.

Instruments: 7-string nylon guitar, custom Neurofunk-oscillator banks, live percussion (bongos played with rubber mallets), and a 40-piece brass section processed through vintage reel-to-reel tape saturation.

For Fans Of: Arca, Irakere, Max Richter, Noisia.

